Method 1

$mat(
  delim: #none,
  2 (2 cos^2 theta - 1) &= 1;
  4 cos^2 theta - 2 &= 1;
  cos^2 theta &= frac(3, 4);
  cos theta &= plus.minus frac(sqrt(3), 2),
)$

$theta = frac(pi, 6), frac(11 pi, 6)$ when $cos theta = frac(sqrt(3), 2)$.

$theta = frac(5 pi, 6), frac(7 pi, 6)$ when $cos theta = -frac(sqrt(3), 2)$.

Method 2

$mat(
  delim: #none,
  2 (1 - 2 sin^2 theta) &= 1;
  2 - 4 sin^2 theta &= 1;
  sin^2 theta &= frac(1, 4);
  sin theta &= plus.minus frac(1, 2),
)$

$theta = frac(pi, 6), frac(5 pi, 6)$ when $sin theta = frac(1, 2)$.

$theta = frac(7 pi, 6), frac(11 pi, 6)$ when $sin theta = -frac(1, 2)$.

The domain is $x > 5$.

$mat(
  delim: #none,
  log_3((x + 3)(x - 5)) &= 2;
  (x + 3)(x - 5) &= 3^2;
  x^2 - 2x - 15 &= 9;
  x^2 - 2x - 24 &= 0;
  (x - 6)(x + 4) &= 0,
)$

$x = 6$ or $x = -4$. Since $-4$ is outside $x > 5$, it is extraneous. Therefore, $x = 6$.

$mat(
  delim: #none,
  12^2 - 7^2 &= y^2;
  95 &= y^2;
  sin alpha &= -frac(sqrt(95), 12);
  5^2 - 3^2 &= x^2;
  16 &= x^2;
  cos beta &= frac(4, 5),
)$

The signs follow from $alpha$ in quadrant IV and $beta$ in quadrant I.

$mat(
  delim: #none,
  sin(alpha - beta) &= sin alpha cos beta - cos alpha sin beta;
  &= (-frac(sqrt(95), 12))(frac(4, 5)) - (frac(7, 12))(frac(3, 5));
  &= frac(-4 sqrt(95) - 21, 60),
)$

$ csc(alpha - beta) = frac(60, -4 sqrt(95) - 21) $.

$mat(
  delim: #none,
  (2^(-2))^(3x) &= (2^(3))^(x - 3);
  2^(-6x) &= 2^(3x - 9);
  -6x &= 3x - 9;
  -9x &= -9;
  x &= 1,
)$

1 mark for changing to a common base ($frac(1, 2)$ mark for each side)

$frac(1, 2)$ mark for exponent law

$frac(1, 2)$ mark for equating exponents

2 marks

$mat(
  delim: #none,
  p(x) &= a(x + 5)^(2)(x - 1)^(2);
  75 &= a(5)^(2)(-1)^(2);
  75 &= 25a;
  a &= 3,
)$

1 mark for multiplicity of 2 at $x = -5$ and $x = 1$

1 mark for consistent value of $a$

$p(x) = 3(x + 5)^(2)(x - 1)^(2)$

2 marks

$mat(
  delim: #none,
  48 frac(n!, (n - 4)! 4!) &= frac((n + 1)!, ((n + 1) - 4)!);
  48 frac(n!, (n - 4)! 4!) &= frac((n + 1)!, (n - 3)!);
  frac(48, 4!) &= frac((n - 4)! (n + 1)!, (n - 3)! n!);
  frac(48, 24) &= frac((n - 4)! (n + 1)(n)!, (n - 3)(n - 4)!(n)!);
  2 &= frac(n + 1, n - 3);
  2n - 6 &= n + 1;
  n &= 7,
)$

1 mark for substitution into equation ($frac(1, 2)$ mark for each side)

1 mark for factorial expansion

$frac(1, 2)$ mark for simplification of factorials

$frac(1, 2)$ mark for solving for $n$

3 marks

$ mat(
  delim: #none,
  log [(x - 1)(x + 2)] &= 1 "" "1 mark for product law";
  (x - 1)(x + 2) &= 10^(1) "" "1 mark for exponential form";
  x^(2) + x - 2 &= 10;
  x^(2) + x - 12 &= 0;
  (x + 4)(x - 3) &= 0,
) $

#strike[$x = -4$]  $x = 3$

$frac(1, 2)$ mark for the permissible value of $x$

$frac(1, 2)$ mark for showing the rejection of the extraneous root

